<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td><span style="white-space:nowrap;"><strong>Assertion&nbsp;(A):</strong></span></td><td>For small difference in absolute temperature, compared with the temperature itself, the net rate of transfer of energy from a body to its surrounding by radiation is directly proportional to the temperature difference.</td></tr><tr><td><span style="white-space:nowrap;"><strong>Reason&nbsp;(R):</strong></span></td><td>The net ratio of transfer of energy by radiation from a body (temperature <span class="math-tex">\(T_b=T_S+\theta\)</span>) to its surrounding (temperature <span class="math-tex">\(T_S\)</span>) is given by:<br> <span class="math-tex">\(\dfrac{dQ}{dt}=eA\sigma(T_b^4-T_S^4)\\ ~~~~~~~=eA\sigma\left\{(T_S+\theta)^4-T_S^4\right\}\\ ~~~~~~~\approx4eA\sigma T^3_S\theta,~\text{where }\theta\ll T_S.\)</span></td></tr></tbody></table>
&nbsp;

